<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tetraevangelion of Ivan Alexander</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1355–1356</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as London Gospels</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tetraevangelion of Ivan Alexander is an illuminated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> of the four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospels">Gospels</a> made in 1355–1356 for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsar">Tsar</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-alexander-of-bulgaria">Ivan Alexander of Bulgaria</a>. Written in Middle Bulgarian by the monk Simeon, it contains several hundred miniatures, including portraits of the tsar and his family, and is the finest surviving work of medieval Bulgarian book art. It was brought from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-athos">Mount Athos</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in the nineteenth century and is now in the British Library.</p></div>
